        return x > y ? 1 : x < y ? -1 : 0;
      }

After the quick-fix is applied:


      public int compare(int x, int y) {
        return Integer.compare(x, y);
      }


Note that `Double.compare` and `Float.compare` slightly change the code semantics. In particular,
they make `-0.0` and `0.0` distinguishable (`Double.compare(-0.0, 0.0)` yields -1).
Also, they consistently process `NaN` value. In most of the cases, this semantics change actually improves the
