the method is called, and m is the number of elements in the collection passed to the method as a parameter.
When the list is large, this can be an expensive operation.


The quick-fix wraps the list in `new java.util.HashSet<>()` since the time required to create
`java.util.HashSet` from `java.util.List` and execute `containsAll()` on
`java.util.HashSet` is O(n+m).

**Example:**

